include <string>;

"\section=1"
"\name=headers"
"\summary=add SPDX header to git repository source files"

namespace headers
{
  {
    "<options> <dir> <expression>",

    "\h|SYNOPSIS|

     \c{\b{headers} [<options>] [<dir>]}

     \h|DESCRIPTION|

     The \cb{headers} command adds the SPDX header to the source files of a
     git repository. The header names the copyright holder and the license of
     the file and is written in the comment syntax of the file type, for
     example:

     \
     // Copyright (c) the IW4x authors (see the AUTHORS file).
     // SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-only WITH AdditionRef-IW4x-Exception-1.1
     \

     The license expression comes from the \cb{license} value of the
     repository manifest or, in a multi-package repository, of its package
     manifests, which must agree. The IW4x license (\cb{other: GPL-3.0-only
     with IW4x Linking Exception 1.1}) is written as the expression above and
     an SPDX license expression is written as is. A repository without a
     manifest needs the \cb{--license} option.

     If <dir> is specified, then it should be a directory inside the working
     tree of the repository to update or, with \cb{--all}, the directory to
     search for repositories. Otherwise, the current working directory is
     assumed.

     Every file git tracks is either a source file, which must have the
     header, or a file that cannot or need not carry one (see FILE TYPES
     below for details). Symbolic links and submodules are skipped and a file
     with the \cb{spdx} attribute unset is excluded, which is how third-party
     and generated files are kept out. For example, in \cb{.gitattributes}:

     \
     deps/** -spdx
     \

     The header is only written into a file that carries no notice at all. A
     file whose leading comment already mentions a copyright or license is
     listed for review: it is either excluded or given the header by hand,
     next to its notice, which is then accepted. Notices are never removed or
     rewritten.

     The command fails if a file is of an unknown type, if a file is to be
     reviewed, or if a header is incomplete or names another license
     expression, listing each file, for example:

     \
     error: files in '/src/hello' carry notices of their own
       info: libs/zlib/zlib.h
       info: exclude third-party files with -spdx in .gitattributes
       info: or add the header next to the notice by hand
     \

     The update proceeds the same way as that of \cb{mailmap(1)}:

     \ol|

     \li|Prepare every repository: establish its license expression, inspect
     its files, and establish that the files can be changed, committed, and,
     if requested, pushed. If any repository fails to prepare, then nothing
     is changed in any of them.|

     \li|Print the plan: for each repository with files missing the header,
     the license expression, the numbers of source and excluded files, where
     the commit is pushed to, and the files to add the header to.|

     \li|Ask for confirmation, unless \cb{--yes} is specified.|

     \li|Add the header to each file, installing it with a single rename, then
     commit and, if requested, push the files.||

     For example:

     \
     $ headers

     repository:   /src/hello
     license:      GPL-3.0-only WITH AdditionRef-IW4x-Exception-1.1
     source files: 2 (0 with the header)
     excluded:     1

     add the header to:
       hello/buildfile
       hello/hello.cxx

     add the header to 2 files in '/src/hello'? [y/n]: y
     \

     The commit subject is \cb{Add SPDX headers}.

     \h#file-types|FILE TYPES|

     The type of a file is established from its name and extension and the
     header is written in the comment syntax of the type:

     \dl|

     \li|\cb{//}

     C, C++, CLI, Protocol Buffers, resource scripts, and the other C-like
     languages.|

     \li|\cb{#}

     \cb{buildfile}, \cb{*.build}, \cb{testscript}, shell, Python, Perl,
     Ruby, YAML, TOML, CMake, make, PowerShell, PO files, and \cb{.htaccess}.|

     \li|\cb{--}

     Lua and SQL.|

     \li|\cb{/* */}

     CSS, SCSS, and Less.|

     \li|\cb{<!-- -->}

     HTML, XHTML, and XML.|

     \li|\cb{rem}

     Windows batch files.||

     Man pages (\cb{*.1} to \cb{*.9}) take the roff comment, \cb{.\\\"}.

     Documentation (\cb{README}, \cb{NEWS}, Markdown), license texts,
     \cb{AUTHORS}, \cb{LEGAL}, manifests, git configuration files, plain
     text, JSON, and binary files (images, fonts, archives, executables,
     and so on) cannot or need not carry the header.

     A template (\cb{*.in}) has the type of the file it produces and a file
     of otherwise unknown type that starts with a shebang line is a script.

     The header goes at the beginning of the file, after the byte order
     mark, the shebang line, and, in HTML and XML, the XML declaration and
     the document type declaration. It is followed by an empty line (an
     empty comment in a man page) and written with the line endings of the
     file.
     "
  }

  class options
  {
    "\h|OPTIONS|"

    bool --all|-a
    {
      "Operate on every repository found under \i{dir}. Without this option,
       the repository \i{dir} is in is operated on. The search does not
       descend into a repository working tree, so nested repositories
       (submodules, repositories checked out inside another one) are never
       visited. Linked worktrees are skipped as well, since their repository
       is visited through its main working tree. Hidden directories are
       searched."
    };

    std::string --license
    {
      "<expression>",
      "Use the SPDX license expression for the headers of every repository
       operated on, whatever their manifests say. This is necessary for a
       repository without a manifest."
    };

    bool --no-commit
    {
      "Don't commit the files the header is added to. By default, they are
       committed and only they are, whatever else is staged."
    };

    bool --push
    {
      "Push the commit to the upstream of the current branch. To push exactly
       this commit, the branch must not be ahead of its upstream before the
       update. If the push fails, then the commit is kept and the rest of the
       repositories are still updated."
    };

    bool --show-push
    {
      "Print the push command without executing it. This allows examining
       the commit before pushing it. Note that the command is printed to
       \cb{stdout}."
    };

    bool --print-only
    {
      "Print to \cb{stdout} what would be done without actually doing
       anything."
    };

    bool --yes|-y
    {
      "Don't prompt for confirmation before adding the headers."
    };

    bool --help
    {
      "Print usage information and exit."
    };

    bool --version
    {
      "Print version and exit."
    };
  };

  "
  \h|EXIT STATUS|

  Non-zero exit status is returned in case of an error, including when the
  update is not confirmed. Nothing is changed in any repository if the error
  is diagnosed before the confirmation.
  "
}
